menu "app_trust -- who the device believes"

choice APP_TRUST
    prompt "How TLS servers are trusted"
    default APP_TRUST_PRIVATE_CA
    help
        PLAN §3's design fork, and the only decision here that is hard to change once
        devices are deployed -- a device trusts what its firmware says it trusts.

        It applies to every TLS connection the template makes: the OTA server and, when
        it is enabled, the MQTT broker. One anchor for the whole device.

config APP_TRUST_PRIVATE_CA
    bool "A private CA embedded in the firmware"
    help
        The default, and the right answer for a NAS on your own LAN. tools/gen_certs.sh
        makes the CA and the server certificate; server_certs/ca_cert.pem is embedded at
        build time.

        The CA is embedded, never the leaf, so the server certificate can be re-issued
        without reflashing anything. It works with no route to the internet, which is the
        situation these devices are usually in. The cost is that the CA's own expiry
        eventually needs an OTA, and that a device will trust nothing else -- including a
        perfectly valid public certificate.

config APP_TRUST_CERT_BUNDLE
    bool "The ESP-IDF root certificate bundle"
    select MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE
    help
        Trust the public root CAs instead, the way a browser does. Choose this when the
        server has a certificate from a public issuer -- Let's Encrypt via DSM, say, which
        DSM will also renew on its own.

        Rotation becomes somebody else's problem, at the cost of about 64 KB of flash and
        of needing a name that actually resolves. There is no pinning: any certificate any
        public CA issues for that name is accepted, which is a different and larger trust
        boundary than one CA you made yourself.

endchoice

endmenu
